cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let's discuss whether or not 3@C should be a diamond transfer. A few people play that, but that is not my recommendation. More commonly, people either play 4-way transfers. |
PG| ***** 44 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If you want to play 4-way transfers, then 2N is a transfer to diamonds and 2@S is a transfer to clubs. That is what I prefer. However, playing that way means that 2N is no longer an invite. You have to bid |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=stayman,Stayman^*N|CP||FP||at| with an invite which is the disadvantage. |
PG| ***** 45 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The other way to transfer to diamonds is to play 2@S is a transfer to either clubs or diamonds. Then after 2@S bid by responder opener bids 3@C and then responder passes or bids 3@D. I don't like that particularly because it wrong sides the contract in diamonds and is very inflexible. |
PG| ***** 46 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The other problem with playing 3@C as a diamond transfer is that these days many people use 3@C as what is called ^*BPuppet Stayman^*n which allows responder to find out whether opener as a 4-card major or a five card major. |
PG| ***** 47 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|This is becoming common. I don't love that although it has some merit. Personally I like to play 3 level bids are natural slam tries. For example, if North had a slightly stronger hand, I would bid 3@D as a slam try. |
PG| ***** 48 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|On this hand, although the spade void is worrisome I would just bid 3N and hope that either partner has spades stopped and has the @DA and can easily run 9 tricks, or perhaps has spades double stopped, or doesn't get a spade lead.|
PG| ***** 49 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BNorth: ^*NI also thought about that bidding 3NT. Is that okay?|
PG| ***** 50 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|To answer North's question, yes I think 3N is best. Admittedly there are hands where 5@D makes and 3N does not. However, there are more hands where 3N makes and 5@D does not. |
PG| ***** 51 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In general it is almost always easier to make 3N than five of a minor and even in those cases where you should be in five of a minor you often cannot find out that is the case and give the defense too much information about what to lead if you look for five of a minor. |
PG| ***** 52 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|A big advantage of bidding 3N over 1N is it provides little information to the defense regarding the best opening lead.|
PG| ***** 53 ***** |


ub||
PG| ***** 54 ***** |


mb|3N|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 55 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
pc|S2|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|A spade lead is what most players would choose and when the opponents have not looked for a major suit fit a major is often a good lead and might be what I chose as well.|
PG| ***** 56 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However, I do not like to lead top of nothing in a major, although many do. I really prefer 4th from longest and strongest. So I might very well lead a club which might work out better.|
PG| ***** 57 ***** |


